[[File:Mcqueen sarabande final dress.jpg|thumb|Tanya Dziahileva wearing the final look from ''Sarabande'' on the runway. The fresh flowers attached to the dress began to fall off during her walk, creating a moment of serendipitous beauty.{{Sfn|Deniau|2012|p=23}}{{sfn|Bowles|2014|p=176}}|alt=Woman wearing a full-length dress covered with flowers on the outside and at the neck and armholes.]]
'''''Sarabande''''' is the twenty-ninth collection by British fashion designer [[Alexander McQueen]], released for the Spring/Summer 2007 season of his [[Alexander McQueen (brand)|eponymous fashion house]]. ''Sarabande'' was an exploration of fragility and decaying grandeur expressed through floral motifs. It was primarily inspired by [[Barry Lyndon|''Barry Lyndon'']] (1975), a film set in the eighteenth century and known for its themes of [[fatalism]] and [[romanticism]]. Elements of the traditional clothing of Mexico and Spain appeared alongside an exaggerated hourglass silhouette updated from the [[Edwardian era]]. The palette was muted, with washed-out dark colours, off whites, and muted pinks and purples, reflecting traditional English mourning colours. 

The runway show was staged on 6 October 2006 at the [[Cirque d'hiver|Cirque d'Hiver]] in Paris. The set was designed to resemble an abandoned wood-floored theatre with a [[theatre-in-the-round|round stage]]. A [[chamber orchestra]] was seated on the stage in two groups facing one another, with a large glass chandelier hung between them. The orchestra played a new version of a [[sarabande]] by [[George Frideric Handel]] known for appearing in ''Barry Lyndon''; the collection takes its name from the musical style. Forty-six looks were presented. The last dress was covered with silk and fresh flowers, which, having been hastily sewn on, began falling off on the runway, creating what critics regarded as a moment of [[serendipitous]] beauty. 

Response to ''Sarabande'' was positive, with reviewers feeling that it was a successful follow-up to McQueen's previous season. It is recalled as one of his defining collections. Academic analysis has considered the collection's influences and the floral finale dresses. Items from the collection have appeared in exhibitions like the retrospective ''[[Alexander McQueen: Savage Beauty]]''.

== Background ==
British fashion designer [[Alexander McQueen]] was known for his imaginative, sometimes controversial designs, and dramatic [[Fashion show|fashion shows]].<ref name=":172">{{Cite web |title=Alexander McQueen – an introduction |url=https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/alexander-mcqueen-an-introduction |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210317231608/https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/alexander-mcqueen-an-introduction |archive-date=17 March 2021 |access-date=25 June 2024 |website=[[Victoria and Albert Museum]]}}</ref>{{sfn|Mora|Berry|2022|pp=126, 128, 132}} During his nearly twenty-year career, he explored a broad range of ideas and themes, including [[Historicism (art)|historicism]], [[romanticism]], femininity, sexuality, and death.<ref name=":172" />{{sfn|Mora|Berry|2022|pp=126, 128, 132}}{{Sfn|Bolton|2011|pp=13–14}} He learned tailoring as an apprentice on [[Savile Row]] and dressmaking as head designer at French fashion house [[Givenchy]].{{efn|From 1996 to October 2001, McQueen was simultaneously head designer at [[Givenchy]] and at his own label.{{Sfn|Wilcox|2015|p=327}}{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=255}}}}<ref name=":32">{{Cite web |last=Bowles |first=Hamish |author-link=Hamish Bowles |date=15 July 2010 |title=Alexander McQueen: Noble farewell |url=https://www.vogue.com/article/alexander-mcqueen-noble-farewell |access-date=30 January 2025 |website=[[Vogue (magazine)|Vogue]]}}</ref> Although he worked in [[ready-to-wear]] – clothing produced for retail sale – his showpiece designs featured a degree of craftsmanship that verged on ''[[haute couture]]''.<ref name=":42">{{Cite web |last=Socha |first=Miles |date=4 November 2002 |title=King McQueen: A mature Alexander McQueen cast aside the shock values (and even the adolescent rants) to produce a dazzling spring collection. (Movers Shakers) |url=https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=ITOF&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CA94237973&v=2.1&it=r&sid=bookmark-ITOF&asid=6d861079 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230601053346/https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=ITOF&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CA94237973&v=2.1&it=r&sid=bookmark-ITOF&asid=6d861079 |archive-date=1 June 2023 |access-date=31 May 2023 |website=[[Women's Wear Daily]]}}</ref><ref name="vogue2">{{Cite web |last=Mower |first=Sarah |date=9 March 2010 |title=Alexander McQueen Fall 2010 Ready-to-Wear Collection |url=https://www.vogue.com/fashion-shows/fall-2010-ready-to-wear/alexander-mcqueen |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210417204015/https://www.vogue.com/fashion-shows/fall-2010-ready-to-wear/alexander-mcqueen |archive-date=17 April 2021 |access-date=17 January 2025 |website=[[Vogue (magazine)|Vogue]]}}</ref><ref name="menkes">{{Cite news |last=Menkes |first=Suzy |author-link=Suzy Menkes |date=9 March 2010 |title=McQueen's mesmerizing finale |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2010/03/10/fashion/10iht-rmcq.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250114225802/https://www.nytimes.com/2010/03/10/fashion/10iht-rmcq.html |archive-date=14 January 2025 |access-date=6 February 2025 |work=[[The International Herald Tribune]] |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> In 2000, McQueen sold 51 per cent of his company to Italian fashion house [[Gucci]] but retained creative control.<ref>{{cite news |last=Porter |first=Charlie |date=5 December 2000 |title=McQueen move fuels fashion feud |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2000/dec/05/charlieporteronmensfashion.expertopinions |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150926113316/http://www.theguardian.com/uk/2000/dec/05/charlieporteronmensfashion.expertopinions |archive-date=26 September 2015 |access-date=16 March 2021 |work=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref><ref name="bbc obit2">{{Cite news |date=11 February 2010 |title=Obituary: Fashion king Alexander McQueen |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/8511160.stm |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170902202706/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/8511160.stm |archive-date=2 September 2017 |access-date=11 February 2010 |work=[[BBC News]]}}</ref>

McQueen's personal fixations and interests were a throughline in his career, and he returned to certain ideas and visual motifs repeatedly.<ref name="va-intro2">{{Cite web |title=Alexander McQueen – an introduction |url=https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/alexander-mcqueen-an-introduction |access-date=25 June 2024 |website=[[Victoria and Albert Museum]] |archive-date=17 March 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210317231608/https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/alexander-mcqueen-an-introduction |url-status=live }}</ref>{{Sfn|Bolton|2015|p=21}}{{sfn|Wilcox|2015|p=25|ref={{sfnref|Wilcox in Wilcox}}}} His collections were [[Historicism (art)|historicist]], in that he adapted historical designs and narratives, and self-referential, in that he revisited and reworked ideas between collections.<ref name="va-intro">{{Cite web |title=Alexander McQueen – an introduction |url=https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/alexander-mcqueen-an-introduction |access-date=25 June 2024 |website=[[Victoria and Albert Museum]] |archive-date=17 March 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210317231608/https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/alexander-mcqueen-an-introduction |url-status=live }}</ref> He looked to the arts for inspiration, drawing on photographs, paintings, and films.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2011 |title=Art and religion |url=http://www.vam.ac.uk/museumofsavagebeauty/art-and-religion/ |access-date=7 August 2025 |website=The Museum of Savage Beauty |publisher=[[Victoria and Albert Museum]]}}</ref>{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|pp=307–310, 318}} He incorporated his love of the natural world throughout his career with organic materials and visual references.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Alexander McQueen – an introduction |url=https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/alexander-mcqueen-an-introduction |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210317231608/https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/alexander-mcqueen-an-introduction |archive-date=17 March 2021 |access-date=17 January 2023 |website=[[Victoria and Albert Museum]]}}</ref>{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=252}}{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}} Birds and feathers were an ever-present theme.{{Sfn|Fennetaux|2018|loc=7}}<ref name="Met-WOC2">{{Cite web |date=2011 |title=Dress, ''The Horn of Plenty'', Autumn/Winter 2009–10 |url=https://blog.metmuseum.org/alexandermcqueen/dress-horn-of-plenty/ |access-date=21 September 2024 |website=[[Metropolitan Museum of Art]]}}</ref> His favourite flowers, roses, recurred in various forms.{{Sfn|Honigman|2021|p=|pp=38, 110}}{{sfn|Haye|2020|p=25}} They appealed to him in part because of their multi-faceted cultural associations: romance and beauty on the one hand, and pain, blood, and violence on the other.{{sfn|Haye|2020|p=172}} McQueen frequently played with similarly contrasting themes in his work.{{Sfn|Honigman|2021|p=|pp=38, 110}}{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=156}} 

He was also known for playing with the silhouette by cutting or structuring garments to produce unusual shapes. In [[Pantheon ad Lucem|''Pantheon'' ''ad Lucem'']] (Autumn/Winter 2004), for example, he emphasised the hips, but also showed cinched waists and padded jackets.{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=212}} A number of McQueen's collections included variations on a moulded leather bodice that strictly defined and sculpted the torso, all based on [[Lifecasting|life casts]] of model Laura Morgan. The first of these was in ''[[No. 13 (Alexander McQueen collection)|No. 13]]'' (Spring/Summer 1999), followed by [[It's Only a Game (Alexander McQueen collection)|''It's Only a Game'']] (Spring/Summer 2005) and ''[[In Memory of Elizabeth Howe, Salem, 1692]]'' (Autumn/Winter 2007).{{Sfn|Evans|2015|pp=191–192}}

In the years preceding ''Sarabande'', McQueen had reached a stage of exhaustion with his career and the [[fashion industry]], at one point saying, "I go in, I do my business, do the parties, and leave."{{Sfn|Thomas|2015|p=333}} The collections he presented in 2005, ''[[The Man Who Knew Too Much (Alexander McQueen collection)|The Man Who Knew Too Much]]'' (Autumn/Winter 2005) and ''[[Neptune (collection)|Neptune]]'' (Spring/Summer 2006), were criticised for being dull and commercial.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=138}}{{sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|p=31}} Although the previous season's collection, [[The Widows of Culloden|''The Widows of Culloden'']] (Autumn/Winter 2006), had been seen as a return to form, McQueen remained under pressure to make sales.<ref name=":22">{{Cite web |last=Frankel |first=Susannah |author-link=Susannah Frankel |date=4 March 2006 |title=The ghost of Kate Moss looms above McQueen show |url=https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=STND&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CA142827340&v=2.1&it=r&sid=bookmark-STND&asid=286a68f9 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221020052031/https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=STND&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CA142827340&v=2.1&it=r&sid=bookmark-STND&asid=286a68f9 |archive-date=20 October 2022 |access-date=24 October 2022 |website=[[The Independent]]}}</ref><ref name="Telegraph-03-2006">{{Cite news |last=Alexander |first=Hilary |date=4 March 2006 |title=Kinky boots and clan tartan from the Kaiser and McQueen |url=https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=STND&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CA142807294&v=2.1&it=r&sid=bookmark-STND&asid=9ef86a53 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221020040115/https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=STND&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CA142807294&v=2.1&it=r&sid=bookmark-STND&asid=9ef86a53 |archive-date=20 October 2022 |access-date=20 October 2022 |website=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |via=[[Gale]]}}</ref> In 2004, the Gucci group, which owns the McQueen brand, had advised their subsidiary fashion houses that they needed to [[Break-even|break even]] by 2007, so producing a collection that could perform commercially was of utmost importance.<ref name="WWD-Real">{{Cite magazine |last=Socha |first=Miles |date=3 April 2006 |title=The Real McQueen |url=https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=ITOF&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CA147621233&v=2.1&it=r&sid=bookmark-ITOF&asid=10c849a4 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221020052029/https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=ITOF&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CA147621233&v=2.1&it=r&sid=bookmark-ITOF&asid=10c849a4 |archive-date=20 October 2022 |access-date=24 October 2022 |magazine=[[Women's Wear Daily]]}}</ref>{{Sfn|Watt|2012|pp=236–237}}

== Concept and creative process ==
=== Inspiration ===
