''Sarabande'' (Spring/Summer 2007) is the twenty-ninth collection McQueen designed for his eponymous [[Alexander McQueen (brand)|fashion house]]. The concept was an exploration of fragility and decaying grandeur expressed through floral motifs.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}} The name was taken from a style of music called the [[sarabande]], which originated in sixteenth century colonial [[Latin America]] before being adopted in a slower form in Europe.{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=304}}{{Sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|p=153}} According to [[Sarah Burton]], McQueen's [[wikt:right-hand_woman|right-hand woman]], the collection changed drastically during the working process, to a degree she considered unusual. Some items were discarded, while others, such as the [[Tailcoat|tailcoats]], were reworked.{{Sfn|Bolton|2011|p=230}} McQueen later described his work on the collection and its predecessor as his "second wind in fashion", saying that he was enjoying the process more than he had been.<ref name="purple">{{Cite web |last=Zahm |first=Olivier |date=Spring–Summer 2007 |title=Alexander McQueen |url=https://purple.fr/magazine/ss-2007-issue-7/alexander-mcqueen/ |access-date=20 June 2025 |website=[[Purple (magazine)|Purple]] |archive-date=10 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250510231634/https://purple.fr/magazine/ss-2007-issue-7/alexander-mcqueen/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Like much of McQueen's work, ''Sarabande'' drew on McQueen's favourite art.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}} The collection was primarily inspired by [[Barry Lyndon|''Barry Lyndon'']], a 1975 film by [[Stanley Kubrick]] set in the eighteenth century, known for its themes of [[fatalism]] and [[romanticism]].{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=304}}{{Sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|p=153}} It also drew on the life and aesthetics of Italian socialite [[Luisa Casati]] (1881–1957), who was known as a muse for artists, going so far as to declare that she wanted to be "a living work of art."{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=155}} A 1908 portrait of her by [[Giovanni Boldini]], which shows her in a black dress and large matching hat, was especially formative.{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=304}} The designs were also reminiscent of the costumes from the 1971 film [[Death in Venice (film)|''Death in Venice'']], set in the European [[Belle Époque]]; it was one of McQueen's favourites.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}}{{Sfn|Bolton|2011|p=230}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=232}} Early looks blended elements of the [[sailor suit]] worn by Tadzio, a young boy whose beauty is central to the film, and the black-and-white outfits worn by his mother.{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=234}} ''Sarabande'' also drew on Spanish painter [[Francisco Goya]] and the [[Culture of Mexico#Fashion in Mexico|traditional clothing of Mexico]] and Spain.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}}{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=304}}{{Sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|pp=139, 143}}

=== Collection ===
The palette for ''Sarabande'' reflected the clothing worn during traditional English phases of [[mourning]]: shades of black, grey, and off-white, as well as dusky hues of pink, lavender, and mauve, which the Edwardians called "ashes of roses".{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}}{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=158}}{{Sfn|Arnold|2007|p=208}} This gave the collection an antiquated aesthetic, which McQueen compared to hand-tinted sepia prints by Victorian photographer [[Julia Margaret Cameron]].{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}}<ref name="purple" />

McQueen often revisited and refined ideas from earlier shows, and ''Sarabande'' was no exception.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Mower |first=Sarah |date=6 October 2006 |title=McQueen Spring 2007 Ready-to-Wear Collection |url=https://www.vogue.com/fashion-shows/spring-2007-ready-to-wear/alexander-mcqueen |access-date=16 June 2025 |website=[[Vogue (magazine)|Vogue]] |archive-date=3 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250703005708/https://www.vogue.com/fashion-shows/spring-2007-ready-to-wear/alexander-mcqueen |url-status=live }}</ref> His characteristic tailoring appeared in slimline tailored suits, fitted [[Riding jacket|riding jackets]], and narrow dresses.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=155}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=232}}{{Sfn|Homer|2023|pp=100, 104}} Other designs showcased his interest in adapting and modernising historical styles. References to clothing of the eighteenth century and the [[Edwardian era]] were mediated through McQueen's love of the costume design from ''Barry Lyndon''.{{Sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|p=|pp=139, 143, 153}}{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=228}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=|pp=232–234}} The historicist designs were updated with modern flourishes such as asymmetry, especially a suite of [[High-low skirt|high-low skirts]] with hems cut at calf length in the front and left long in the back.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=156}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=235}}

''Sarabande'' relied heavily on nature imagery for effect, especially [[English landscape garden|English garden]] flowers, a contrast to McQueen's usual preference for depicting the natural world untouched by humanity.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}}{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=228}} His depiction of florals for this collection was somewhat morbid. A few months after the show, he told ''Harper's Bazaar'' that he had been thinking of a series of photographs of dying fruit by his friend [[Sam Taylor-Wood]]: "Things rot. It was all about decay. I used flowers because they die."<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Frankel |first=Susannah |author-link=Susannah Frankel |date=31 March 2007 |title=The real McQueen |url=https://www.harpersbazaar.com/fashion/designers/a68/alexander-mcqueen-0407/ |access-date=16 June 2025 |magazine=[[Harper's Bazaar]] |archive-date=7 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240707021440/https://www.harpersbazaar.com/fashion/designers/a68/alexander-mcqueen-0407/ |url-status=live }}</ref>{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}} Some garments had embroidery of flowers, while some had full silk or fresh flowers stitched to them.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}}{{Sfn|Honigman|2021|p=38}} A few dresses had petals trapped beneath chiffon, a style which evoked designs by Koji Tatsuno, a mentor of McQueen's.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}} McQueen referenced Spanish designer [[Cristóbal Balenciaga]] in a set of dresses with puff skirts, especially Look 43, which has a three-tiered puff skirt that author Judith Watt described as "resembling an overblown exotic fungi".{{Sfn|Watt|2012|pp=235–236}} Birds and feathers appeared in delicate prints and embroidery, reflecting the ornithological art of [[John James Audubon]].{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}}{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=156, 158}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=234}} One gown was covered entirely in feathers.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=158}}

