The collection focused on an exaggerated Edwardian [[Hourglass figure|hourglass silhouette]] with cinched waists and emphasised hips, which McQueen felt enhanced the natural sensuality of the female body.<ref name="purple" />{{Sfn|Honigman|2021|p=30}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=|pp=232–234}} There were several "infanta" dresses, which have tight bodices and wide-hipped skirts; Balenciaga originated this style, drawing on Spanish court costume of the seventeenth century.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=155}}<ref>{{Citation |title="Velasquez" |date=1939 |url=https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/84758 |access-date=8 June 2025 |archive-date=8 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250608161028/https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/84758 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":0" /> For example, Look 18, a black lace dress with broad hips, echoes the wide-hipped silhouette common in the eighteenth century. The historical style was created by [[Pannier (clothing)|panniers]], a type of stiff framework worn beneath skirts, but McQueen's version uses soft padding instead.{{Sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|p=153}} Other historicist flourishes include lace, [[Corset|corsets]], and [[Puff sleeve|puffed sleeves]] and shoulders.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=155}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=232}}<ref name=":0" />

Because of its multi-faceted inspiration, many elements can be seen as a reference to more than one source. The collection's ruffled shirts resembled men's shirts of the eighteenth century, as seen in ''Barry Lyndon'', but were also reminiscent of traditional Spanish and Mexican clothing, which often feature ruffles and flounces.{{Sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|pp=139, 143}}{{Sfn|Homer|2023|p=104}} ''Sarabande'' revisited ideas from McQueen's earlier collection [[The Dance of the Twisted Bull|''The Dance of the Twisted Bull'']] (Spring/Summer 2002), which had been heavily inspired by Spanish [[matadors]].{{Sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|pp=139, 143}} ''Sarabande'' featured a short series of black-and-white ensembles with ruffled shirts and embroidery reminiscent of Spanish [[blackwork]] lace. Two of these had tight cropped trousers like the matador's ''[[taleguilla]]'', and one had beadwork that visually resembled the matador's traditional [[Suspenders|braces]].{{Sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|pp=139, 143}}

McQueen worked with other creatives to produce things outside his area of expertise for runway shows, relying on longtime collaborators [[Philip Treacy]] and [[Shaun Leane (jeweller)|Shaun Leane]] for headpieces and jewellery, respectively.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web |title=McQueen's collaborators: Shaun Leane |url=https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/mcqueens-collaborators-shaun-leane |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240203073518/https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/mcqueens-collaborators-shaun-leane |archive-date=3 February 2024 |access-date=25 January 2024 |website=[[Victoria and Albert Museum]]}}</ref> For ''Sarabande'', Treacy created exaggerated hats in black silk, evoking oversized roses or the shape of the [[Chaperon (headgear)|chaperon]] from the [[Jan van Eyck]] painting known as [[Portrait of a Man (Self Portrait?)|''Portrait of a Man in a Turban'']] (1433), which was beloved by McQueen's friend and muse [[Isabella Blow]].{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=235}}{{sfn|Haye|2020|p=196}} Jewellery for the collection was fairly minimal. Look 21 featured a silver choker with rose thorns, drawing on similar items he had created for McQueen's ''[[Dante (collection)|Dante]]'' (Autumn/Winter 1996).<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |date=20 September 2020 |title=Shaun Leane for Alexander McQueen rose thorn choker, Sarabande collection, Spring-Summer 2007 |url=https://www.kerrytaylorauctions.com/auction/lot/lot-52---shaun-leane-for-alexander-mcqueen-rose-thorn-choker-sarabande-collection-spring-summer-2007/?lot=27897 |access-date=23 June 2025 |website=[[Kerry Taylor Auctions]]}}</ref> There were also earrings and chokers with [[Cameo (carving)|cameos]] and hair, reflecting Victorian [[Mourning jewelry|mourning jewellery]], which often included hair as a memento of the deceased. Early in his career, McQueen had referenced this practice by encasing locks of his own hair in the labels and linings of some garments.<ref name=":3" />{{Sfn|Townsend|2015|p=|pp=159, 162}}{{Sfn|Bolton|2011|p=235}} 

{{Clear|left}}

=== Showpiece ensembles ===
{{Quote box
| quote = I liked the padded hips because they didn’t make the dress look historical, but it made it look more sensual. Like the statue of [the Roman goddess] [[Diana (mythology)|Diana]] with breasts and big hips. It’s more maternal, more womanly.
| source = Alexander McQueen, 2007, interview with ''[[Purple (magazine)|Purple]]''<ref name=purple/>
| align = right
| width = 20%
}}

Look 39 was a cream-coloured leather dress, hand-painted with birds and floral designs. Its rigid torso was moulded from Morgan's life casts, complete with breast shapes, abdominal muscles, and navel.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}}{{Sfn|Evans|2015|p=192}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=235}} Watt described the shape as having a "raw sexuality", tempered by the delicate paintwork.{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=235}}

Look 26, a short white dress, was paired on the runway with a take on a [[mantilla]], a traditional Spanish veil, in grey rhinestone-accented lace. It was draped over a high [[flower crown]] and wrapped around the body like a cloak.{{Sfn|Worsley|2009|p=189}} Curator Kate Bethune found it reminiscent of a 1922 portrait of Casati by [[Ignacio Zuloaga]].{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}} Katherine Knox wrote that it was "McQueen's take on a Victorian bride", but with a Gothic undertone that suggested "something sinister lurking beneath the [[Veil|bridal veil]]".{{Sfn|Knox|2010|p=74}}

The last two ensembles, Looks 45 and 46, were companions. Look 45 was a knee-length mauve silk dress with [[Bell sleeve|bell sleeves]] and a wide [[boat neck]], all filled with flowers in muted colours. A tightly cinched waist and padded hips created the collection's signature hourglass silhouette. The structural elements of the skirt were visible beneath the sheer fabric.{{Sfn|Honigman|2021|pp=110–111}} The final piece, Look 46, was a nude silk organza gown sleeveless gown with a high collar. The body of the dress was covered with silk and fresh flowers, which, like in Look 45, also filled the inside of the neckline and sleeves.{{Sfn|Honigman|2021|p=38}}<ref name="Met-WOC">{{Cite web |date=2011 |title=Sarabande |url=https://blog.metmuseum.org/alexandermcqueen/tag/sarabande/ |access-date=29 May 2025 |website=[[Metropolitan Museum of Art]]}}</ref> Taylor-Wood's dying fruit series was a major inspiration for these items, as was ''Garden'' (2000), an [[art installation]] by [[Marc Quinn]] which involved preserved frozen flowers.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}} 

== Runway show ==
The runway show was staged on 6 October 2006 at the [[Cirque d'hiver|Cirque d'Hiver]] in Paris.{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=345}} McQueen typically worked with a consistent creative team for his shows. His longtime collaborator Katy England was responsible for overall styling.{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=345}} Gainsbury & Whiting oversaw production.{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=345}} Joseph Bennett, who had designed all of McQueen's runways since [[No. 13 (Alexander McQueen collection)|''No. 13'']] (Spring/Summer 1999), took care of [[set design]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=16 March 2015 |title=Interview: Joseph Bennett on Lee McQueen |url=https://www.showstudio.com/projects/unseen_mcqueen/interview_joseph_bennett?autoplay=1 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240215021422/https://www.showstudio.com/projects/unseen_mcqueen/interview_joseph_bennett?autoplay=1 |archive-date=15 February 2024 |access-date=14 February 2024 |website=[[SHOWstudio]]}}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite web |title=Day 2: Joseph Bennett |url=https://www.showstudio.com/projects/tumblr-takeover-joseph-bennett/day-2 |access-date=28 February 2025 |website=[[SHOWstudio]]}}</ref> [[Eugene Souleiman]] styled hair, while [[Charlotte Tilbury]] handled make-up.{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=345}} 

The soundtrack to the show comprised "Sarabande in Black", a new version of the fourth-movement sarabande from "[[Keyboard suite in D minor (HWV 437)|Keyboard suite in D minor]]" by [[George Frideric Handel]], which was featured in ''Barry Lyndon''.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}}<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Sheppard |first=Philip |title=Alexander McQueen – Sarabande |url=https://www.philipsheppard.com/project/alexander-mcqueen-sarabande/ |access-date=22 June 2025 |website=Philip Sheppard Projects}}</ref> "Sarabande in Black" was composed by [[Philip Sheppard (musician)|Philip Sheppard]].<ref name=":1" /> Compared to Handel's original, Sheppard's version is musically [[Inversion (music)|inverted]] and concludes with an instrumental arrangement of "[[Paint It Black|Paint it Black]]" (1966) by [[The Rolling Stones]]. It was played live at the runway show by the [[Academy of St Martin in the Fields]] chamber orchestra, overlaid with the sound of [[Lancaster bomber|Lancaster bombers]].<ref name=":1" />{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=161}}{{Sfn|O'Neill|2015|p=263}} 

Styling for models was melancholy, with [[Mourning veil|funeral veils]] and unnaturally white faces.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}} The skin tone was created by mixing white pigment with [[Foundation (cosmetics)|foundation]], which was then brushed onto the face and neck, leaving deliberate visible brushstrokes on the neck and collarbone.{{Sfn|Miller|2015|p=235}} Hair appeared to be haphazardly pinned up, with wisps coming loose.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=155}} Some had braids similar to those in the mother-and-child portrait ''Mexico City'' (1934) by [[Henri Cartier-Bresson]].{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}}

The set was designed to resemble an abandoned theatre, with the audience seated around the wood-floored circular stage in an [[theatre-in-the-round|in-the-round]] configuration.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}}{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=304}} The orchestra was seated on the stage in two groups facing one another, wearing grey dresses and suits.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=156}}{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=304}}{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=233}} A large glass chandelier hung at floor level between them, draped with fake cobwebs and torn chiffon to make it appear old and abandoned.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=155}}

{{Quote box
| quote = I recall the expression on his face in 2006 when the orchestra played Handel's "Sarabande" as the last dress, made entirely of roses and not quite finished, sewn together at the very last minute, left a trail of loose flowers along the catwalk{{nbsp}}[...] It could have been a disaster; instead, it was a small miracle, backstage was full of ecstatic joy.
| source = Photographer [[Anne Deniau]], reflecting on ''Sarabande'' in her book ''Love Looks Not With the Eyes'' (2012){{sfn|Deniau|2012|p=23}}
| align = right
| width = 20%
}}

The show opened with the lit chandelier rising to the ceiling with the rest of the room in darkness, reminiscent of a scene from ''Barry Lyndon''.{{Sfn|Bethune|2015|p=318}}{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=304}} The lights went up when it reached its apex, and models began to enter through a draped opening at the rear of the stage.{{sfn|Alexander McQueen|2012|loc=0:08–0:34}} The models walked in a figure eight around the two orchestra groups before exiting.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=156}} McQueen's usual stage theatrics were kept to a minimum, apart from the chandelier and orchestra.{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=228}}

Forty-six looks were presented, opening with a focus on tailored or slimline looks before turning to designs with exaggerated hips and hourglass silhouettes at Look 18.{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=234}} The collection closed with a strong emhasis on floral motifs and dresses were covered or filled with flowers.{{Sfn|Wilson|2015|p=305}} Look 46, the last dress, was covered with silk and fresh flowers, which were sewn on just before the model walked out onto the runway. Loosely attached, the flowers began falling off onto the runway while the model was walking. Although many audience members assumed this had been carefully staged, the effect was completely accidental, and has been described as a moment of [[serendipitous]] beauty.{{Sfn|Deniau|2012|p=23}}{{sfn|Bowles|2014|p=176}} 

After the final model exited, the lights went down briefly, leaving only the chandelier lit while the orchestra continued to play.{{sfn|Alexander McQueen|2012|loc=12:55–13:28}} The lights came back up and the models came out for their final turn, re-ordered to reflect the traditional English sequence of mourning colours: first, black ensembles for full mourning, then mixed grey and off-white looks for secondary mourning, and finally, dusky pink, lavender, and mauve for half-mourning.{{Sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|p=153}}{{Sfn|Arnold|2007|p=208}} McQueen came out for his bow while they were walking.{{sfn|Alexander McQueen|2012|loc=14:18–14:30}} The lights dimmed again as they exited for the final time. The orchestra concluded in darkness and exited the stage.<ref name=":0" />{{sfn|Alexander McQueen|2012|loc=15:08–15:42}}   

== Reception ==
[[File:McQueen, Musée des beaux-arts - 51.jpg|thumb|Look 23 at ''Mind, Mythos, Muse'']]
''Sarabande'' was well-received by contemporary reviewers, who saw it as a successful follow-up to the previous season's critically lauded ''Widows of Culloden''.{{Sfn|Gleason|2012|p=161}} Sarah Mower of ''[[Vogue (magazine)|Vogue]]'' felt that McQueen had balanced artistry with commercial considerations, which he had not always managed in the past.<ref name=":0" /> She called the collection "a revision of all the things the designer does best", highlighting wearable, modern items such as an asymmetrical tunic with ruffles of pink lace as standouts.<ref name=":0" /> Olivier Zahm of ''[[Purple (magazine)|Purple]]'' magazine wrote that the clothes in ''Sarabande'' "invoke the grace, elegance, and underlying sexuality of bygone eras, as if to mask in fragile beauty the violent masochism of today".<ref name="purple" />

Imogen Fox of ''[[The Guardian]]'' was more ambivalent. Although she appreciated the show's portrayal of "eerie decaying grandeur", the dresses seemed unrealistic to her, "more like costume" than wearable fashion. Conversely, she felt the tailored jackets were wearable and would continue to be commercially popular.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Fox |first=Imogen |date=7 October 2006 |title=McQueen's tailoring wins wearability war |url=https://link.gale.com/apps/doc/A152478864/ITOF?u=wikipedia&sid=bookmark-ITOF&xid=13f359fa |access-date=22 June 2025 |work=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref>

The flower-filled dresses garnered much attention. Although the flowers falling off the final dress was a serendipitous effect, many critics have taken it as a demonstration of the fragility of life.{{Sfn|Homer|2023|p=104}} Knox called the flower-filled dresses "on par with [[Haute couture|''couture'']] sensibilities" in terms of their craftsmanship.{{Sfn|Knox|2010|p=74}} Watt wrote that the final look was "one of his most exaggerated and thought-provoking pieces".{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=236}} Ana Finel Honigman identified the final gown as one of McQueen's signature garments.{{Sfn|Honigman|2021|p=38}}

The collection is well-regarded in retrospect. Curator [[Claire Wilcox]] wrote that in the collection, "function and beauty were disdained for a luxurious impracticality and historicist beauty".{{Sfn|Fairer|Wilcox|2016|p=228}} Sarah Burton recalled it as one of McQueen's most iconic shows.{{Sfn|Bowles|2014|p=176}} Watt called it "near-perfect".{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=232}} In 2015, ''[[I-D]]'' magazine deemed the floral finale gown one of the defining looks of McQueen's career.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McKimm |first=Alastair |date=14 April 2015 |title=The eight looks that defined Alexander McQueen's career |url=https://i-d.co/article/the-eight-looks-that-defined-alexander-mcqueens-career/ |access-date=18 May 2025 |website=[[i-D]] |archive-date=28 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250528123332/https://i-d.co/article/the-eight-looks-that-defined-alexander-mcqueens-career/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

