== Legacy ==
[[File:Bergdorf Goodman (5) (cropped).jpg|thumb|Red Rose dress by Sarah Burton for Alexander McQueen, Autumn/Winter 2019]]
The final gown was reconstructed after the show, the fresh flowers replaced by silk reconstructions by Blumen.{{sfn|Haye|2020|p=196}} The [[Sarabande Foundation]], a charity established by McQueen and funded by his estate, takes its name from this collection.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Garrahan |first=Rachel |date=2 April 2018 |title=Alexander McQueen's legacy: The Sarabande Foundation |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/02/style/alexander-mcqueen-sarabande-foundation-london.html |access-date=20 June 2025 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> Following McQueen's suicide in 2010, Burton succeeded him as creative director of the brand.<ref name=":5" /><ref name=":6" /> Roses continued to be a recurring motif for the brand under her leadership; they reminded Burton of young women being chosen as the [[Rose Queen]] at spring festivals during her childhood.{{sfn|Haye|2020|p=|pp=196, 198}} 

The [[Metropolitan Museum of Art]] (the Met) in [[New York City]] owns Look 22, a white cocktail dress with black tulle overlay.<ref name="Met">{{Cite web |date= |title=Alexander McQueen {{!}} Dress {{!}} British |url=https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/139296 |access-date=29 May 2025 |website=[[Metropolitan Museum of Art]]}}</ref> The [[Victoria and Albert Museum]] of London owns a grey pinstriped wool pantsuit from the collection.<ref>{{Cite web |date=1997 |title=Jumpsuit |url=https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O84303/jumpsuit-mcqueen-alexander/ |access-date=29 May 2025 |website=[[Victoria and Albert Museum]] |archive-date=28 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250528123424/https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O84303/jumpsuit-mcqueen-alexander/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The [[National Gallery of Victoria]] owns Look 12, an ensemble comprising a blouse and a skirt with bird print, and Look 20, a black evening dress.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Look 12, blouse and skirt |url=https://www.ngv.vic.gov.au/explore/collection/work/148641/ |access-date=29 May 2025 |website=[[National Gallery of Victoria]] |archive-date=25 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221025023636/https://www.ngv.vic.gov.au/explore/collection/work/148641/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Evening dress |url=https://www.ngv.vic.gov.au/explore/collection/work/145455/ |access-date=29 May 2025 |website=[[National Gallery of Victoria]] |archive-date=27 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250527053333/https://www.ngv.vic.gov.au/explore/collection/work/145455/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The [[Los Angeles County Museum of Art]] (LACMA) owns several items from the collection: a jacket, two dresses, three blouses, two skirts, a pair of trousers, and three pairs of shoes.{{Sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|pp=170, 172}}

Socialite [[Daphne Guinness]] was photographed by [[Sølve Sundsbø]] wearing Look 41, a full-length gown in lilac tulle with padded hips.{{Sfn|Watt|2012|p=232, 288}} [[Tim Walker]] photographed Look 43 for a ''Vogue'' editorial, while [[Paolo Roversi]] photographed Look 9 for another.{{Sfn|Fox|2012|p=119}} [[Annabelle Neilson]], a model and friend of McQueen's, posed for Lee Jenkins wearing Look 42 for the magazine's March 2007 issue.{{Sfn|Fox|2012|p=135}} [[Paris Jackson]] wore a copy of Look 20, a black lace off-the-shoulder dress, in the [[music video]] for her song "[[Let Down (Paris Jackson song)|Let Down]]" (2020).<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nordstrom |first=Leigh |date=13 November 2020 |title=Paris Jackson blends Alexander McQueen with Radiohead for debut album |url=https://wwd.com/eye/people/paris-jackson-debut-album-alexander-mcqueen-archives1234657508-1234657508/ |access-date=23 June 2025 |website=[[Women's Wear Daily]] |archive-date=8 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241208064331/https://wwd.com/eye/people/paris-jackson-debut-album-alexander-mcqueen-archives1234657508-1234657508/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Neilson died in 2018; in 2020, her collection of McQueen designs was auctioned, including several items from ''Sarabande''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hiort |first=Astrid |date=4 March 2020 |title=Archive of McQueen muse Annabelle Neilson to be auctioned |url=https://www.showstudio.com/news/archive-mcqueen-muse-annabelle-neilson-be-auctioned |access-date=23 June 2025 |website=[[SHOWstudio]]}}</ref> Her copy of Look 20 sold for £15,600, a jacket with veil from Look 21 sold for £1,820, and the rose thorn choker from the same look sold for £10,400.<ref name=":3" /><ref>{{Cite web |title=Alexander McQueen black lace 'pannier' evening dress, 'Sarabande', Spring-Summer 2007 |url=https://www.kerrytaylorauctions.com/auction/lot/lot-47---alexander-mcqueen-black-lace-pannier-evening-dress-sarabande-spring-summer-2007/?lot=27890&sd=1 |date=20 September 2020 |access-date=23 June 2025 |website=[[Kerry Taylor Auctions]] }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Alexander McQueen black crêpe jacket with draped veil, 'Sarabande', Spring-Summer 2007 |url=https://www.kerrytaylorauctions.com/auction/lot/lot-51---alexander-mcqueen-black-crpe-jacket-with-draped-veil-sarabande-spring-summer-2007/?lot=27896 |date=20 September 2020 |access-date=23 June 2025 |website=[[Kerry Taylor Auctions]] }}</ref> Fashion dealer Steven Philip auctioned his collection in 2023, including a number of archival McQueen pieces.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Goldstone |first=Violet |date=9 June 2023 |title=Coveted John Galliano, Vivienne Westwood pieces go up for auction |url=https://wwd.com/fashion-news/fashion-features/coveted-john-galliano-vivienne-westwood-clothing-for-auction-1235678275/ |access-date=20 September 2024 |website=[[Women's Wear Daily]] |archive-date=21 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231021204240/https://wwd.com/fashion-news/fashion-features/coveted-john-galliano-vivienne-westwood-clothing-for-auction-1235678275/ |url-status=live }}</ref> A black and ivory ruffled gown from ''Sarabande'', a variation of Look 10 from the runway, sold for £7,800.<ref name=":02">{{Cite web |date=20 June 2023 |title=Lot 134 – A pair of rare Alexander McQueen gold snakeskin heels |url=https://www.kerrytaylorauctions.com/auction/lot/lot-134---a-pair-of-rare-alexander-mcqueen-gold-snakeskin-heels-widows-of-culloden-collection-autumn-winter-2007-08/?lot=33608&so=0&st=widows&sto=0&au=113&ef=&et=&ic=False&sd=1&pp=48&pn=1&g=1 |access-date=20 September 2024 |website=[[Kerry Taylor Auctions]] |archive-date=21 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240921012830/https://www.kerrytaylorauctions.com/auction/lot/lot-134---a-pair-of-rare-alexander-mcqueen-gold-snakeskin-heels-widows-of-culloden-collection-autumn-winter-2007-08/?lot=33608&so=0&st=widows&sto=0&au=113&ef=&et=&ic=False&sd=1&pp=48&pn=1&g=1 |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Exhibitions ===
Look 21, a hooded black suit paired with a Shaun Leane thorn necklace, appeared in ''Gothic: Dark Glamour'', a 2008 exhibition at the [[Fashion Institute of Technology|Museum at FIT]] in New York.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2008 |title=McQueen at night |url=https://sites.fitnyc.edu/depts/museum/Gothic/Mcqueen.thorns.html |access-date=5 August 2025 |website=[[Fashion Institute of Technology]]}}</ref>

The 2011 exhibition ''[[Alexander McQueen: Savage Beauty]]'', originally at the [[Metropolitan Museum of Art]], featured four ensembles from ''Sarabande'': Look 25, a white knee-length dress with black lace overlay and vertically puffed sleeves; Look 39, the white leather moulded dress hand-painted with birds; and looks 45 and 46, the final two gowns filled with flowers.<ref name="Met-WOC" />{{Sfn|Bolton|2011|p=234}} The accessories section of the exhibit featured a pair of earrings with [[Cameo (carving)|cameos]], with matching locket on black ribbon, and the white and tan pony-skin corset from Look 8.{{Sfn|Bolton|2011|p=235}}

The final gown was a centrepiece of a 2019 exhibition called ''Roses'' at the Alexander McQueen [[Old Bond Street]] store. It was displayed alongside the Red Rose dress by Burton from the brand's Autumn/Winter 2019 collection.<ref name=":5">{{Cite web |last=Moss |first=Jack |date=2 December 2019 |title=This new exhibition explores Alexander McQueen's love of flowers |url=https://www.anothermag.com/fashion-beauty/12121/this-new-exhibition-explores-alexander-mcqueens-love-of-flowers-roses-2019-2020 |access-date=29 May 2025 |website=[[AnOther]]}}</ref><ref name=":6">{{Cite web |date=29 November 2019 |title=Alexander McQueen ''Roses'' exhibition presents a garden of knowledge |url=https://www.showstudio.com/news/alexander-mcqueen-roses-exhibition-presents-garden-knowledge |access-date=29 May 2025 |website=[[SHOWstudio]] |archive-date=27 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250527053332/https://www.showstudio.com/news/alexander-mcqueen-roses-exhibition-presents-garden-knowledge |url-status=live }}</ref> The items owned by the LACMA appeared in the exhibition ''Lee Alexander McQueen: Mind, Mythos, Muse'', originally staged in 2022.{{Sfn|Esguerra|Hansen|2022|pp=170–171}}
